{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789253668,"nonce":"5k8ELBfJpEd4xAjZhp5xgTCoaYs=","title":"Python: a heap value passed to a function is never released","message":"Every heap-typed parameter of a Python function is ParamOwnership::Owned\n(3939655), because Python code may keep anything it is handed. The\ndrop pass therefore never releases a box, list, dict or instance after\nit is passed to a Python function, and nothing else does either. A\nscript that builds a temporary in a loop and passes it to a helper\ngrows without bound.\n\nTwo ways out, and the design allows both: an escape analysis over the\ncallee body that downgrades a parameter to Borrowed when the body\nneither stores nor returns nor boxes it (the common case for helpers\nthat only read), or opting Python programs into a collector from the\nGC menu. The first is the default path and should land first; the\nsecond is the answer for programs that build cyclic or long-lived\nstructures.","files":null}]}